// --- DISCUSSION (interpretation) ---

=== Perceived workload

Descriptively, participants reported higher workload for mobile swipe typing than for laptop keyboard typing, particularly on mental demand, effort, and frustration. One plausible explanation is the additional planning and correction demands of gesture-based word entry, although typing speed was not jointly modeled with TLX in this analysis. Low frustration ratings for laptop keyboard typing may also reflect greater familiarity with physical keyboards for short replies.

Differences between formal- and informal-assigned participants should be interpreted cautiously. Prompt register was manipulated between subjects with unequal group sizes (five vs. thirteen participants with TLX), so workload differences may reflect participant composition rather than prompt style alone. Confirmatory conclusions would require mixed-effects models with participant random effects and, ideally, inferential comparison of modalities.
